---
import PublicLayout from "../layouts/PublicLayout.astro";
import Waitlist from "../components/Waitlist.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<PublicLayout title="Why Lore — Project memory for your existing coding agent" description="Decide whether Lore fits your workflow: existing coding agents, local history, reviewable project knowledge, and Git-based sharing.">
 <section class="section page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Why Lore</p><h1>Keep the decisions behind the code. <em>Use the agent you already have.</em></h1><p class="hero-desc">Lore fits projects where the same reasoning gets explained again: why an approach was rejected, which convention matters, or what broke last time.</p><a class="button button-primary" href={`${base}docs/install/`}>Choose your setup →</a></section>
 <nav class="page-sections section" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#pillars">Is it a fit?</a><a href="#landscape">Compare approaches</a><a href="#cost">Data &amp; costs</a><a href="#portability">What’s available</a></nav>
 <section class="section" id="pillars"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Start with the job you need done.</h2></div><dl class="capabilities"><div><dt>Continue work across sessions</dt><dd>Keep searchable history and project knowledge when a conversation gets long or you start another session.</dd></div><div><dt>Inspect what was learned</dt><dd>Read the knowledge and its source, correct inaccurate entries, and review the <code>.lore.md</code> diff.</dd></div><div><dt>Carry conventions through Git</dt><dd>Share the reasoning alongside code changes, with a pull request that a teammate can review.</dd></div></dl></section>
 <section class="section" id="landscape"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Choose the approach that matches your workflow.</h2><p>Different tools serve different starting points. This is a guide to evaluating fit, rather than an exhaustive feature scorecard.</p></div>
 <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Approach comparison"><table><caption>Where to start when evaluating project memory</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Your situation</th><th scope="col">Useful starting point</th><th scope="col">What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><th scope="row">A small set of stable rules</th><td>A hand-maintained <code>AGENTS.md</code> or client instruction file</td><td>Can your team keep it accurate and concise without automatic history or curation?</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Memory inside an application you are building</th><td>Memory infrastructure such as <a href="https://docs.mem0.ai/platform/quickstart">Mem0</a> or <a href="https://supermemory.ai/docs">Supermemory</a></td><td>Do its SDK/API, deployment options, data boundaries, and retrieval behavior fit your application?</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Building agents and workflows in TypeScript</th><td>An agent framework such as <a href="https://mastra.ai/docs">Mastra</a>, with memory as part of its stack</td><td>Do you want to own the agent implementation, tools, and workflow?</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">Project memory for an existing coding agent</th><td>Lore’s gateway and supported integrations</td><td>Does your client work with the connection path, and do reviewable Markdown knowledge and local history suit your workflow?</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div><p class="small muted">Official documentation linked above was checked on 27 October 2026. These products can overlap; the rows describe starting points and do not imply that any tool lacks other capabilities.</p></section>
 <section class="section" id="context-engine"><span id="depth" class="fragment-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="section-heading"><h2>Storage is only one part of the workflow.</h2><p>Lore records history, distills older working context, and curates project knowledge. What reaches a request is selected within scope and context limits. Your agent can use recall to look up details beyond that material.</p></div><a class="text-link" href={`${base}docs/memory/`}>Understand stored history and current context →</a></section>
 <section class="section" id="cost"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Know the extra work before a long session.</h2><p>Distillation, curation, and conversation imports can make model calls. Provider pricing, model choices, and configuration affect their cost. Inspect recorded estimates in the local app and compare them with your provider’s actual usage.</p></div><p>Local SQLite storage does not mean requests to a remote model provider stay on your machine. Production operational telemetry is enabled by default and has an opt-out.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}docs/data-and-costs/`}>See the data flow, telemetry choice, and cost views →</a></section>
 <section class="section" id="portability"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Three ways of keeping and sharing memory.</h2></div><dl class="capabilities"><div><dt>Local Lore · Available now</dt><dd>Recorded sessions, context management, recall, and curated project knowledge through supported clients.</dd></div><div><dt>Git-based sharing · Available now</dt><dd>Commit and review <code>.lore.md</code>. It shares the knowledge file, not the full conversation database.</dd></div><div><dt>Folk Lore · Early access</dt><dd>The upcoming hosted team experience for continuous sharing. Joining the list expresses interest; it does not enable sync.</dd></div></dl><a class="text-link" href={`${base}docs/team-memory/`}>Review the current Git workflow →</a></section>
 <section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Try a result you can inspect.</h2><p>Follow a webhook decision from a conversation into a reviewed knowledge entry and a later task. The example separates a recorded message, extracted knowledge, and successful recall.</p></div><a class="button button-primary" href={`${base}docs/example/`}>Try the sample workflow →</a><p><a href={`${base}docs/benchmark/`}>Read the historical benchmark and its limitations</a></p></section>
 <span id="team" class="fragment-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span><Waitlist />
</PublicLayout>
